<!-- ── Debrief (shown after completion) ── -->
<div class="lab-debrief" id="lab-debrief">
  <div class="lab-debrief-label">Debrief — Race Condition (TOCTOU)</div>

  <h3>What happened</h3>
  <p>GiftRush's redemption endpoint performs two operations sequentially: (1) read the balance and check if it is sufficient, then (2) calculate the new balance and write it. Between these two operations, there is no lock — so concurrent requests all read the <em>same</em> original balance, all pass the check, and all perform a deduction. The result is a balance that goes negative, meaning value was spent that didn't exist.</p>

  <h3>The vulnerable code</h3>
  <pre>// Step 1: Read balance (Time of Check)
const card = await db.getCard(cardId);
if (card.balance < amount) return 400;

// ⚠ No lock — gap between check and use
// Step 2: Write new balance (Time of Use)
await db.updateBalance(cardId, card.balance - amount);</pre>

  <h3>The fix</h3>
  <p>Make the check-and-deduct operation atomic. Use a single SQL statement with a <code>WHERE</code> clause that includes the balance check, or use database-level locks (<code>SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</code>). Implement idempotency keys to prevent duplicate requests from being processed.</p>
  <pre>// Atomic: check and deduct in one statement
const result = await db.query(
  'UPDATE cards SET balance = balance - ? WHERE id = ? AND balance >= ?',
  [amount, cardId, amount]
);

// If affectedRows is 0, the balance was insufficient
// No TOCTOU window — the database handles concurrency

// Also: use SELECT ... FOR UPDATE for complex flows
// Also: implement idempotency keys per request</pre>

  <h3>Real-world impact</h3>
  <p>Race conditions in financial operations allow attackers to create value from nothing — redeeming gift cards multiple times, withdrawing more than their balance, applying discount codes repeatedly, or exploiting any check-then-act flow. These bugs are often missed in testing because they only manifest under specific timing conditions.</p>
  <p><strong>Starbucks (2015):</strong> Security researcher Egor Homakov discovered a race condition in the Starbucks gift card API that allowed double-spending balances by sending concurrent redemption requests. By exploiting the TOCTOU window between the balance check and the deduction, an attacker could transfer or redeem the same balance multiple times before the system updated.</p>

  <h3>How to find this in the wild</h3>
  <p>Look for any endpoint that reads a value, makes a decision based on it, and then writes a new value — especially financial operations, coupon redemptions, and vote/like systems. Use tools like Turbo Intruder (Burp Suite extension) to send dozens of identical requests with precise timing. Compare the expected final state with the actual state. If the balance is lower (or higher) than expected, you have a race condition.</p>

